            <ArticleTitle>A double-blind comparison of codeine and morphine for postoperative analgesia following intracranial surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1029-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Codeine and morphine were compared in a double-blind study of postoperative analgesia in 40 patients after intracranial neurosurgery. Eighteen patients received codeine phosphate 60 mg and 18 morphine sulphate 10 mg, both by intramuscular injection; 4 patients (10%) required no analgesia. Both drugs provided analgesia within 20 min of injection but morphine was more effective than codeine beyond 60 min (p = 0.01). Fewer doses of morphine than codeine were required (p = 0.003). Nine patients requested one dose of morphine and 9 two doses. Seven patients required three doses of codeine and 1 patient required four doses. Neither drug caused respiratory depression, sedation, pupillary constriction or unwanted cardiovascular effects. We conclude that, in the doses used, morphine is a safe alternative to codeine for analgesia after neurosurgery and has a more persistent action.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
